# Why every setting, env var, header and command this gateway uses is here.
#
# The rule, from the published methodology: a setting may appear only if it is needed to boot the
# process at all, to point an upstream at the test mock instead of a real provider, to expose an
# ingress path the matrix exercises, or to bind the port and cores the rig requires. One claim per
# line: <pattern> <boot|upstream|ingress|bind>. Anything unclaimed is a lint failure, and a claim
# that matches nothing is a stale claim and also a failure.
#
# Migrated from GW_CONFIG_WHY in gateway.sh, which is dead code the lint was still reading.
providers      upstream  # the provider table
openai         upstream  # one entry per mock-reachable dialect; Bifrost resolves the provider from
anthropic      upstream  # the request model name, so this is what makes an egress column addressable
gemini         upstream
cohere         upstream
keys           boot      # Bifrost will not register a provider with no key entry (dummy value)
network_config upstream  # base_url -> the mock
# Claimed because the dead gateway.sh launcher still passes it and the lint reads that file. The
# LIVE config does not set it: the harness sets it for every gateway from the cores it pinned
# (manifest.rs's pinned_parallelism), so no entrant states its own.
GOMAXPROCS     bind      # Go pre-1.25 reads the HOST cpu count, not the --cpuset-cpus limit

Native ingress routes require /anthropic, /genai, /cohere and /bedrock prefixes in transports/v2.5.0 (69f4d56d18b9ba00ac5f9cf969b4501a882b9967), transports/bifrost-http/integrations/{anthropic,genai,cohere,bedrock}.go. Bedrock cross-provider ingress uses a URL-encoded provider/model ID; without that prefix Bifrost chooses its unconfigured Bedrock upstream. The whole Bedrock upstream column remains untestable because the plain-HTTP mock cannot replace its HTTPS endpoint. Direct pinned-image probes and observed upstream dialects are retained under results/diagnostics/refresh-configuration/bifrost-native-route-probe-2026-11-08.json. A successful HTTP status alone does not prove the requested upstream dialect; the frozen engine re-verification still withholds mismatches.

bench-chat upstream # OpenAI-compatible provider restricted to Chat Completions operations, ensuring the openai column actually uses that wire
bench-responses upstream # Separate Responses-only provider, ensuring the Responses column does not silently reuse Chat Completions
custom_provider_config upstream # Public custom-provider operation selection; core/providers/openai/openai.go supports Responses-to-Chat fallback when Responses is disabled

The final provider configuration passes 30 exact upstream and response checks in bifrost-final-config-correctness-preflight.json. The earlier 28-cell result omitted Chat/Cohere-to-Responses because gpt-4o-mini is also Chat-capable in the model catalog. Declaring the Responses-only model and its documented conversion switch exposes both cells. No gateway source was modified.

The two allowed_requests maps describe distinct mock upstream operations. The shipped shouldFallbackResponsesToChat path activates only when a custom Chat backend lacks Responses; without that declaration a Responses request goes to /v1/responses and cannot measure the Chat column. Request limits, caching, retries and pool sizing retain their defaults. Source: https://github.com/maximhq/bifrost/blob/69f4d56d18b9ba00ac5f9cf969b4501a882b9967/core/providers/openai/openai.go

